Load Testing — a walkthrough you can repeat
1. The core idea
A load test answers one question: "at what request rate does this system stop meeting its promise, and why?"

You do it in a loop:


 pick a flow  →  write a script that drives it  →  ramp load until it breaks
       ↑                                                      │
       │                                                      ▼
   prove the fix  ←  apply a fix  ←  find WHY it broke (observability)
Everything below is that loop, with the exact files and commands.

2. Phase 0 — decide what you're testing before touching k6
Three decisions, on paper:

a) The flow. One user-visible action that exercises the code path you care about. Ours: POST /events (it fans out gateway → identity → event-service → tenant + rule-engine → Postgres → RabbitMQ). Pick the write path — it touches the most services.

b) The load model. This is the #1 mistake people make.

Model	k6 executor	What it does	Use it to…
Closed	ramping-vus	N virtual users, each waits for its response before sending the next	see realistic per-user latency; it self-limits — hides the cliff
Open	ramping-arrival-rate	fire X req/s no matter how slow responses are	find the breaking point — the backlog builds and the knee is obvious
We use open (mainflow-open.js) to find the edge, closed (mainflow-closed.js) for the "what a user feels" number.

c) The SLO. The promise. Without it "broken" is subjective. Ours: p95 < 300 ms, error rate < 1 %.

3. Phase 1 — the k6 script (anatomy)
A k6 script is 4 parts. Open loadtest/mainflow-open.js alongside this:


// (1) METRICS you care about — custom, on top of k6's built-ins
const ingest = new Trend("ingest_latency", true);   // a latency distribution
const ingestErr = new Rate("ingest_errors");        // a 0..1 failure fraction

// (2) OPTIONS — the shape of the load + the pass/fail rules
export const options = {
  scenarios: { edge: {
    executor: "ramping-arrival-rate",
    startRate: 2, timeUnit: "1s",
    preAllocatedVUs: 20, maxVUs: 60,   // VU pool k6 draws from to hit the rate
    stages: [ {target:3,duration:"30s"}, {target:6,...}, ... ],  // the ramp
  }},
  thresholds: {
    ingest_latency: ["p(95)<300"],     // marks the run ✗ if crossed
    http_req_failed: [{ threshold:"rate<0.40", abortOnFail:true, delayAbortEval:"1m" }], // kill switch
  },
};

// (3) setup() — runs ONCE, return value is passed to every iteration
export function setup() { return provisionTenant({ api: API }); }  // register+tenant+rule

// (4) default fn — ONE iteration = one unit of load
export default function (data) {
  const res = http.post(`${API}/events`, JSON.stringify({...}),
    { headers: {...}, tags: { step: "ingest" } });   // tags let you slice metrics later
  ingest.add(res.timings.duration);
  ingestErr.add(res.status >= 400);
}
Supporting files:

loadtest/lib/setup.js — the shared provisionTenant() (register → tenant → enabled rule, all fatal on failure). Reused by every scenario. Lesson: put per-run fixture creation in setup(), not in the iteration — it runs once, and its return value is the only thing shared.
loadtest/smoke.sh — run this first, always. Curls the flow once by hand. If POST /events isn't 201 here, the k6 run measures garbage. 5 seconds saves 30 minutes.
loadtest/hold.js — constant-arrival-rate at a fixed painful rate. Not for finding the edge — for sitting in the broken state while you screenshot / diagnose.
4. Phase 2 — baseline run

bash loadtest/smoke.sh                                  # sanity
kubectl scale deploy --all -n ai-notification --replicas=1   # known state
caffeinate -i k6 run --summary-export=loadtest/out/baseline-open.json loadtest/mainflow-open.js
caffeinate -i = don't let the Mac sleep mid-run (a sleep gave us a bogus max=17m40s).

How to read the k6 summary — the 6 lines that matter:

Line	Meaning	Bad sign
http_reqs ...... 6.7/s	delivered rate	far below your target → system can't keep up
dropped_iterations 1917	iterations k6 wanted to start but couldn't (VU pool exhausted)	any large number → you've passed the edge
ingest_latency p(95)	your SLO metric	crosses your threshold → knee
http_req_failed 29%	non-2xx fraction	rising → knee
iteration_duration vs http_req_duration	former excludes sleep/think-time	huge gap → sleep pollution or setup cost
✓/✗ thresholds	pass/fail verdict	—
The knee = the ramp step where p95 crosses the SLO and errors start climbing. That step's rate is your baseline capacity. Ours: ~2–4 req/s.

5. Phase 3 — diagnose why (the observability triangle)
Four tools, each answers a different question. Run loadtest/hold.js at a rate past the knee so the system sits broken, then look:

Tool	URL / command	Answers
k6 output	the run itself	is it broken, and how (latency vs errors vs drops)
kubectl top pods	kubectl top pods -n ai-notification --sort-by=cpu	which pod is starved (CPU near its limit)
Grafana	http://localhost:9093/d/phase-f-loadtest	rate / latency / error trends over time; CPU vs limit; throttle ratio
Jaeger	http://localhost:9095/search → service, Min Duration 3s → slowest trace	where inside a slow request the time goes (which span)
Decision rules — read the pod that's hottest, then the trace:


pod CPU ≈ its limit  +  throttle ratio > 0.2   → CPU-bound. Fix = more replicas (HPA) / higher limit / less work per request
pod CPU low, pod mem ≈ limit / OOMKilled        → memory-bound. Fix = raise mem / fix a leak
pod CPU low, Postgres "too many clients"        → connection-pool bound. Fix = PgBouncer / connection_limit
pod CPU low, RabbitMQ backlog climbing          → consumer-throughput bound. Fix = more consumers (KEDA on queue depth)
pod CPU low, latency in ONE downstream span     → that downstream service is the real bottleneck — go test IT
pod CPU low, latency in dns.lookup / tcp.connect → connection churn — no channel pooling / no DNS cache
Prometheus (http://localhost:9094/graph) is for raw ad-hoc queries when Grafana doesn't have the panel — e.g. rate(container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total{namespace="ai-notification"}[1m]) / rate(container_cpu_cfs_periods_total[1m]).

Note we had to add the kubelet scrape to Prometheus (envs/state/prometheus-config.yaml) — the Floci setup didn't scrape pod CPU. Lesson: check your metric coverage before you trust "no data" = "not the problem".

6. Phase 4 — fix, Phase 5 — prove
Fix: kubectl apply -f loadtest/hpa/api-gateway.yaml (CPU HPA), or loadtest/keda/channel-service.yaml (queue-depth KEDA). Verify kubectl get hpa shows a real TARGETS %, not <unknown>.
Prove: re-run the exact same k6 command → --summary-export=loadtest/out/hpa-open.json. Compare the two JSON files: sustained req/s at SLO, p95 at 2× baseline load, peak replicas.
Then find the new edge — the bottleneck always moves (scale the gateway → event-service becomes the wall → Postgres → …). Document the chain.
7. How we actually nailed this bottleneck (the reasoning trail)
k6: median 87 ms but p95 8 s and 29 % 504s at only ~6.7 req/s. → Single requests fine, concurrency kills it. Not a code bug (median is fine).
kubectl top during the hold: event-service 234m/250m, api-gateway 203m/250m, everyone else <90m. → CPU-bound, at those two pods.
Postgres (diag.sh): 18/100 connections. RabbitMQ: backlog only because consumers were scaled to 0. → DB and broker ruled out.
Jaeger slowest POST /events trace: the 1.7 s was almost entirely grpc Event/CreateEvent. Inside it, Tenant/CheckMembers = 491 ms but tenant-service's own span = 19 ms; Rule/HasMatchingRule = 205 ms but rule-engine's own span = 9 ms. The gap was dns.lookup (100 ms) + tcp.connect (200 ms). → A new TCP connection + DNS lookup per gRPC call — no channel pooling.
Conclusion: two stacked causes — (a) 250m CPU cap, (b) per-call connection churn. HPA helps (a) by spreading load; the real fix for (b) is gRPC channel reuse + DNS caching + a token cache on the auth hop.
Each tool eliminated a hypothesis. That's the skill: hypothesis → which tool disproves it → next hypothesis.

8. Now build your own — checklist
To load-test a different flow / find a different bottleneck:

Pick the flow. bash loadtest/smoke.sh style: curl it once, confirm the happy path and the exact request/response shapes.
Copy mainflow-open.js → myflow-open.js. Change: the setup() fixtures (in lib/setup.js or inline), the http.* call in default, the custom Trend/Rate names, the tags.
Set the SLO in thresholds.
Guess the bottleneck and make sure you can see it:
CPU/mem → kubectl top + the Grafana CPU panels (already wired).
a queue → add rabbitmq_queue_* panel / rabbitmqctl list_queues.
a DB → psql -c "select ... from pg_stat_activity" (in diag.sh).
a downstream HTTP dep → Jaeger, or add its /metrics scrape to prometheus-config.yaml + curl -X POST localhost:9094/-/reload.
something with no metric → add the exporter/scrape first, or you'll be blind.
Baseline at min replicas, caffeinate -i k6 run --summary-export=... myflow-open.js. Find the knee.
Hold past the knee (hold.js pattern), run your diagnosis snapshot (diag.sh pattern), apply the decision rules in §5.
Fix → re-run same command → diff the summary JSONs → find the new edge.
Golden rules:

One variable per run. Change the fix or the load, never both.
Open model to find the edge, closed model for the UX number.
smoke.sh before every k6 run.
"No data" ≠ "not the bottleneck" — verify the metric exists.
The bottleneck moves. You're never done after one fix.